course: religion-ethics
lesson: 86
title: Keeping promises
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A promise is your word given for the future: when you say "I will", other people plan around it and count on you.
explain: Ava promised to help her little brother build his Lego castle on Saturday morning, then her mate texts "cinema??? 10am"; a more fun plan is not a reason to break her word, though she can honestly ask him to swap days.
explain: A promise you meant but couldn't keep because of a real emergency is not a lie, but it still needs an apology and making up for it; a promise to hide wrongdoing does not bind you at all.
explain: Christians believe God always keeps his promises ("he who promised is faithful", Hebrews 10:23), and Psalm 15 praises the one "who keeps an oath even when it hurts" (Psalm 15:4).
explain: The wise way is to promise only what lies within your power, be clear about when, and keep small promises faithfully even when nobody would find out.
fact: Psalm 15 praises the one "who keeps an oath even when it hurts" (Psalm 15:4).
fact: A promise is a real duty: only a stronger duty, like helping someone who is hurt, can outweigh it, not a better offer.
fact: Christians believe God always keeps his promises ("he who promised is faithful", Hebrews 10:23); a promise broken by a real emergency, honestly explained, is not a lie.
fact: A promise to hide wrongdoing does not bind you; Herod kept a wicked oath, and it cost John the Baptist his life (Mark 6:22-28).
fact: "Like a broken tooth or a lame foot is reliance on the unfaithful in a time of trouble" (Proverbs 25:19); children who see promises broken again and again learn not to hope.
fact: "It is better not to make a vow than to make one and not fulfil it" (Ecclesiastes 5:5).
fact: Fair play in a team means that if you share the fun and the wins, you share the hard work too.
fact: "A trustworthy person keeps a secret" (Proverbs 11:13); being trusted to keep something safe is an honour.
fact: "Well done, good and faithful servant! You have been faithful with a few things" (Matthew 25:21): tell even if no one would find out you slipped.
fact: When life changes a lot, like losing a job, the promiser should explain honestly, and the other person should try to understand; love "is not self-seeking" (1 Corinthians 13:5).
fact: James says to speak of the future with "If it is the Lord's will" (James 4:13-15); promise only what lies within your power.
fact: Words like "now now" (South Africa: soon, in a bit) change from place to place; when it matters, say exactly when.
fact: Ruth promised Naomi, "Where you go I will go" (Ruth 1:16); going home to her own people would have been easier, but she kept her word, stayed with Naomi and became the great-grandmother of King David (Ruth 4:17).
fact: Slang check: "howay" (Newcastle) means come on; "bare" (London) means lots of.
exercise: Ava promised her little brother Lego on Saturday, then gets a cinema invite. What is the best choice? | Keep it, or ask him honestly to swap days | Go to the cinema and say nothing to him about it | Tell him she's feeling ill so she can go out instead | Do the Lego for five minutes, then rush off to the film
exercise: What does a promise create? | A real duty, outweighed only by a stronger duty | A wish that you can drop as soon as a better offer comes along | A rule that only grown-ups have to follow | Nothing at all, unless it is written down
exercise: Dad promised to come to Ellie's play but had a work emergency. Was it a lie? | No; he meant it, so it's a failure that needs an apology, not a lie | Yes; every broken promise is always a lie, whatever the reason for it | Yes, because dads should never work late | No, because promises to children don't count
exercise: Kyle pinky-promised not to tell Mum about the window. Mum asks him straight out. What should he do? | Not lie; a promise to hide wrongdoing doesn't bind him | Lie, because a pinky promise is the strongest kind there is and can never be broken | Run away so he doesn't have to answer | Blame somebody else for breaking the window
exercise: What happened when Herod kept a wicked oath? | It cost John the Baptist his life | He became the richest king in the world | Everyone praised him for being faithful | Nothing, because oaths never matter
exercise: Gemma's da says, "Howay, I'll take ye to the match, promise," for the fifth time. What does "howay" mean? | Come on | Go away | Hurry up | No way
exercise: What can Gemma do about promises that keep getting broken? | Tell her dad kindly that she's stopped believing them | Never speak to her dad again until he finally keeps one | Pretend she doesn't care about football | Make promises she never keeps either
exercise: Josh promised Coach he'd come to every training, but it's raining. What is fair? | Go, or tell Coach honestly in advance | Just don't turn up, and say nothing to Coach about it | Turn up late on purpose and then leave early | Tell Coach he's feeling ill when he isn't really
exercise: What does fair play in a team mean? | If you share a team's fun and wins, you share its work too | Only the captain has to come to training | You only play fair if the others play fair | You play only when it's sunny
exercise: Priya's brother wants to read the diary she's keeping safe: "Bare jokes in there." What should she do? | Keep it closed and keep her brother off it | Read it together, but only the funny bits, as a treat | Let him read it because it's only a diary | Post the best jokes in the group chat
exercise: Arjun forgot to feed the neighbours' cat on Wednesday. Nobody knows. What should he do? | Tell them, so they can check on the cat | Say nothing, because nobody knows | Feed it double on Thursday and keep quiet | Blame the cat for hiding
exercise: Darnell's mum promised a bike, then lost her job. Was it a lie? | No; life changed, and her honest explanation matters most | Yes; she should have known she was going to lose her job soon | Yes; all parents' promises are lies | No, because bikes are not important
exercise: Mia asks Ella to promise to be best friends "for ever and ever". What is the honest, warm reply? | I'll always be kind to you, and I hope we're friends for ever | I promise, cross my heart, for ever and ever | No way, I'll probably get a new friend next year | I can't promise anything to anybody
exercise: Thandi says she'll send it "now now", meaning in a little while. Her London friend expects right now. What helps? | Saying exactly when, like "in ten minutes" | Saying "now now now" to make it faster | Never sending anything to London friends, as they don't understand | Getting cross with the friend for waiting
exercise: What did Ruth promise Naomi (Ruth 1:16)? | Where you go I will go | I will come back next year | I will send you money every month | I will find you a new husband
exercise: What does Ecclesiastes 5:5 say? | Better not to make a vow than to make one and not fulfil it | Make as many vows as you can every day, because vows please God | Vows are only for kings and priests | Break a vow if you get a better offer
test: Your mate says, "Promises to little brothers don't count, he's only six." What is the best reply? | They count the same; he's counting on you, maybe even more | He's right, promises only count with friends who are your own age | Promises to brothers count only on birthdays | Little kids forget, so you can always break them
test: "Pinky promise or I'll batter you," says Kyle's brother about the broken window. Mum asks Kyle. What is the best move? | Tell his brother he won't lie for him, and give him a chance to own up first | Lie to Mum, because a pinky promise is sacred and has to be kept no matter what | Tell Mum and then laugh at his brother | Say "I don't know" and pretend he wasn't there
test: Darnell shouts, "You liar, you promised me a bike!" when his mum lost her job. What is the fairest way to see it? | It's OK to be sad, but it wasn't a lie; she meant it and life changed | He's right; she lied on purpose to trick him | He should never believe his mum again | She should borrow money to buy the bike anyway
test: What would happen if nobody's promises could be relied on? | Nobody could plan anything, like matches, play dates or teams | Life would be more exciting, because nobody would know what comes next | People would make more promises than ever | Nothing; promises don't really matter
test: Thandi's friend says, "You said now now, and it's been an hour, you liar!" What is the best way to see it? | It was a misunderstanding about the words, not a lie; clear words help | Thandi lied on purpose, and she should be punished for it straight away | The friend should learn to wait for ever | "Now now" always means the next day
test: In Newcastle Gemma moans, "Howay man, Da, you always say that." What does she feel? | She doesn't believe the promise any more, because it's been broken so often | She is really excited about going to the match with her dad again this weekend | She wants her dad to go away | She doesn't like football at all
test: Arjun's friend says, "The cat's fine, probably, just keep quiet." What's wrong with that? | The neighbours may need to check, and admitting a slip keeps the trust | Nothing; cats can always look after themselves | It's fine as long as he doesn't forget twice | He should blame the neighbours for leaving
test: Oscar can't think of a promise he kept when it was hard. What would the vicar likely suggest? | Start with a small promise this week and keep it | Never make any promises at all, so you can never break one | Copy someone else's story for Sunday school and say it's yours | Promise something really huge to make up for it all at once
test: A cartoon shows a hero who promises a villain to help steal a treasure, and keeps the promise "because promises are sacred". What is wrong? | A promise to do something wrong does not bind you | Nothing; promises must be kept, whatever they are | The hero should have stolen two treasures | Heroes never make promises in cartoons
test: Your cousin says, "I'll come to every training, 100%," and you know he hates rain. What is the wise advice? | Promise only what you can really do | Promise even more, so Coach is happy | Never go to training at all, so you can't let anyone down | Promise, and then just see what happens
test: The vicar asks, "Why is Ruth's promise beautiful?" What is the best answer? | She kept it when going home would have been easier | She never had to give anything up | She was paid a lot of money by Naomi's family to keep it | It was the only promise she ever made
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the moral status of a promise as set forth in this lesson? | A promise establishes a genuine duty, defeasible only by a weightier duty | A promise establishes no duty whatsoever unless recorded in writing | A promise may be set aside whenever a more agreeable alternative arises | A promise binds the promisee but not the promisor
exam: Given that a promise has been broken in consequence of a genuine emergency, identify the statement consistent with this lesson. | The breach does not constitute a falsehood, though an apology and recompense are owed | The breach constitutes a deliberate falsehood of the gravest kind | The breach requires neither explanation nor apology | The breach releases the promisor from all future obligations
exam: Select the response consistent with the account recorded in Mark 6:22-28. | The keeping of a wicked oath by Herod resulted in the death of John the Baptist | The breaking of a solemn oath by Herod resulted in the death of John the Baptist | Herod declined to swear any oath on that occasion | John the Baptist released Herod from his oath
exam: Ascertain the principal reason for which a pupil ought to inform neighbours that their cat was not fed, notwithstanding that the omission is unknown to them. | The welfare of the animal may require attention, and candour preserves the neighbours' trust | The neighbours are certain to discover the omission in any event | The omission is of no consequence and requires no mention | The pupil is thereby relieved of all further duties for the week
